//# #2745: a match ARM's binder assigned into a slot read later.
//
// Codex, P1 round 12 on #2761. Measured on `7101f3e`: `{ let mut y = idf(a);
// match a { b => { y = b; 0 } }; y }` printed a 3-digit pointer decimal.
//
// The absorb walk -- the half that records what an assignment left in a name --
// masked every arm binder with "" while `dtd_erased_interp_arms`, the value
// walk's twin, PAIRED it against the scrutinee. So `b` was blank, `y = b`
// carried no evidence, and the only thing that could say `y` is erased said
// nothing. One question, two walks, and the pairing now lives in exactly one
// place (`dtd_interp_bind_pat_into`) that both read.
//
// `idf` is a nested lambda, so `let mut y = idf(a)` is the initializer this
// walk declines -- which is what leaves the ASSIGNMENT as the only evidence and
// so isolates this from the initializer routes that already answer.
//
// ROUND 15 NOTE -- this fixture is no longer isolated. `idf` was chosen as an
// initializer this walk DECLINES, so the route named above was the only thing
// that could refuse the program. Round 15 taught the walk to classify a call to
// a local generic lambda, so the initializer alone now refuses it too (measured:
// `{ let mut y = idf(a); y }` is refused on its own). The file still guards
// against the program compiling; it no longer attributes the refusal to this
// route. Re-isolating it needs a T-typed expression the pass cannot classify,
// and the supply of those shrinks every round -- which is #2773's argument, and
// the reason this note is here rather than a hunt for a more obscure spelling.

struct Qt {
  w: Int
}

fn outer[T](x: T) -> String {
  let inner = [T](a: T) -> String {
    let idf = [U](u: U) -> U { u }
    "\{ { let mut y = idf(a)
    match a {
      b => { y = b
        0 }
    }
    y } }"
  }
  inner(Qt::{ w: 5 })
}

export fn _start() -> Int {
  String::length(outer(7))
}
